## Structure

- Package-by-component: capabilities as folders under `lib/components/<name>/`, each fronted by
  `<name>.dart`, which `export`s the public API with `show`. Everything else is under `src/`.
- Clean layers nest inside `src/`: `domain/` `application/` `infrastructure/` `presentation/`.
- Shared technical modules under `lib/shared/<module>/`, same facade-and-`src/` shape.
- App wiring under `lib/app/` — `injection.dart`, `router.dart` — and the composition root in
  `lib/main.dart`, one `lib/main_<env>.dart` per extra environment. These import facades only.

## Layer rules

- Dependencies point inward only. Interfaces are `abstract interface class`es in `application/`
  with the use cases that need them; `domain/` = entities + value objects + errors.
- No `package:flutter`, `flutter_riverpod`, `go_router`, `get_it`, `dio`, `retrofit`, or a storage
  driver in `domain/` or `application/`. `package:injectable` annotations are the one exception.
- Widgets and notifiers are thin; use cases never see a `BuildContext`, a `Ref`, or a DTO.

## DI

- `get_it` registered by `injectable`. `@injectable` on use cases, `@LazySingleton(as: Interface)`
  on implementations, `@module` for third-party objects, `@Environment` to switch them.
  `configureDependencies(environment:)` is called once, in the composition root.
- Constructor injection, interfaces only. `Thing` / `<Qualifier>Thing` naming — `OrderRepository`,
  `RemoteOrderRepository`, `DefaultClock`. No `I` prefix.
- **`GetIt.I` is called in two places and no others**: the composition root, and one bridge
  provider per use case in `presentation/`:

  ```dart
  @riverpod
  CreateOrder createOrder(Ref ref) => GetIt.I<CreateOrder>();
  ```

- Tests pass hand-written fakes to the same constructors. Notifier and widget tests override the
  bridge providers. One smoke test resolves every use case from the container per environment.

## State

- Server → Riverpod async providers · navigation → go_router · shared client → `Notifier` · local →
  `setState` or hooks. Never mirror server or navigation state into a `Notifier`.
- Providers call use cases through the bridge, never gateways. Riverpod 3 forms only; no
  `legacy.dart`. `riverpod_generator` and `riverpod_lint` are on.

## Network and models

- API clients are `retrofit` services on `dio`, in `infrastructure/`, behind a gateway interface in
  `application/`. With an OpenAPI document, `swagger_parser` writes them. Call `dio` directly only
  for an endpoint `retrofit` cannot express.
- Models: `freezed` with `json_serializable` for unions, `copyWith`, and JSON; a plain class with a
  primary constructor when none of those is needed. One `build_runner` command generates
  everything; generated files are committed.

## Enforcement

- `import_lint` in `analysis_options.yaml`, severity `error`, generated for this project. Layer
  rules are wildcarded; facade rules are one per component — adding a component adds its rule.
- Run `flutter analyze`, `dart run import_lint`, `flutter test`, and `dart run build_runner build
  --delete-conflicting-outputs` followed by a clean `git diff`.
- A rule that matches no file reports nothing. Only a deliberate violation proves it.

## Workflow

- Conventional commits, with a body that says *why*.
- Work happens on a branch or worktree, never on `main`. Stacked branches are fine for landing
  large work as smaller reviewable pieces.
- Add a dependency with `flutter pub add <name>`, never by writing a version into `pubspec.yaml`.
- Deliberately minimal: no branching model beyond this is decided here.

## Gotchas

- The analysis server loads `import_lint` at start. After changing `plugins:` restart the IDE; the
  CLI command needs no restart.
- A class nobody annotated is not registered and fails at first `GetIt.I<T>()` on a device. The
  container smoke test is what catches it.
